# vNext Skill: git-commit

## Default runtime role

runtime_role: assistance/v1

Follow `.workflow-system/WORKFLOW_PROTOCOL.md` and the management API in
`.workflow-system/runtime/support/ASSISTANCE_API.md`. Use the native service:
`node .workflow-system/runtime/support/assistance.mjs <command> --root <project>`.
Source-repository equivalent: `node runtime/vnext/support/assistance.mjs`.

Use `task-status` (or the `context.management` view) to identify the task, adopted
plan, step, review/disposition and actual commits. It reads the complete journal,
not one search page or the old CURRENT_TASK tuple. `find`/`read` remain evidence tools.
For task lifecycle changes use `task` actions documented in ASSISTANCE_API; raw
`record` is for general observations, not a substitute for task registration.
Git itself records a commit. A commit request alone requires no task-management write.
For explicitly requested task-management writes, check `recorded`, `association`
and `projection` separately.
If an association/view update fails, retain facts, try a bounded deterministic rebuild
when appropriate, and present concrete link/correct/resolve/defer choices. Honor the
chosen recovery without repeating business actions or asking again for existing consent.
A read-only request uses live task-status without writing a cache. Never declare
full task-management completion merely because a generic record was saved.

If a management operation fails, preserve its actual error and any output already
saved; use another authorized recording method when available and continue
independent authorized work. Do not retry unchanged calls, rerun business commands,
invent IDs/PASS or claim persistence succeeded. Ask only about a genuinely unresolved
user choice or unavailable business prerequisite, not an internal gate code.

## Workflow deviations

Apply **One-time confirmation for workflow deviations** in WORKFLOW_PROTOCOL for
all materially affected actions, not only approvals. Combine known consequences,
reuse an applicable explicit decision, then execute the user's chosen action without
legacy admission or repeated confirmation. Pure management errors need no consent;
report real gaps without making them PASS. Pause only an unresolved user choice.

## Method

Inspect git status and diff, preserve unrelated user changes, and identify the exact
requested commit scope. Only an actual instruction to commit authorizes a commit;
a next_route recommendation is not that instruction. Do not require a Runtime
completed/clean/archived tuple before honoring an authorized commit.

For an ordinary task/stage checkpoint, use the project's **Git checkpoint policy**
from WORKFLOW_PROTOCOL. Within this invocation, run assistance `git-checkpoint`
plan -> authorized setup/staging -> verify-index -> Git commit -> verify-commit.
Follow ASSISTANCE_API's Git checkpoint section for exact inputs and the recipe.
Do not require a separate Skill, archive command or another user turn for these
internal steps. Include project-wide persistent records and recovery material;
do not restrict records to the focused task or wait for tasks to close.
Explicit paths-only/staged-only requests and user exclusions override this default.
Inspect actual staged hunks: the helper checks record bytes and Git path scope,
not whether selected business hunks match the user's intended implementation.
Preserve unrelated/partial staging and custom ignore/attribute policy. Resolve an
actual scope or policy conflict once; never reset the index or force-add to hide it.
Keep plans and verification output in OS temporary files, not in records. Report
saved facts, saved recovery snapshots, locally retained caches, excluded/unknown
files and reference gaps separately. Do not call all residual changes garbage.

Commit only authorized paths and report the actual Git result. Do not push, amend,
reset, clean, force or bypass host security unless specifically authorized. Existing
Git hooks may report their own failures; do not call them Runtime qualification.
After committing, inspect Git read-only and report the actual SHA, committed scope and
remaining working-tree changes. Verify the actual commit against the reviewed index
and checkpoint plan; a mismatch is reported, not auto-amended or recursively repaired.
Do not automatically call `task git`, `record`, `rebuild`
or refresh CURRENT_TASK merely to report this commit. Missing journal Git observations
do not mean the commit is missing and do not require supplementation or another commit.
After a successful commit, read task-status and use its current unfinished stage
for the result's `next_route` (normally `execute-step` for the next step, or
`close-task` when the task has no unfinished step). Do not repeat an earlier
execute-step Git recommendation solely because no `task git` association exists.
If no commit was made, report why and do not imply the Git checkpoint completed.

Use `task` action `git` only when the user explicitly requests a persisted task/execution
association (including an already agreed explicit recording policy). This action writes
management files even though its Git-object check is read-only. Report those writes as
the requested association result; never recursively commit them to record their own SHA.
If that optional write fails, report the real SHA and the association failure separately.

If the requested commit departs from an actual agreed review/test prerequisite, use
one combined consequence confirmation, not a demand to repair Runtime status. Then
commit the authorized files even with disclosed gaps retained in the report. Consent
for this commit neither grants push/deployment nor bypasses host access controls.

## Result

Return the actual requested result, affected scope, evidence/record references,
remaining work and any unsaved/unreconciled data. Preserve existing user decisions
and exclusions; an explicit read-only request remains read-only. A record's successful
write does not certify the product or expand authorization.

Always include `next_route` in the final result: one public Skill ID or `null`,
with a brief reason. Choose it from the actual outcome and remaining work; use
`next_route: null` when no public Skill is justified. Finish any further stages
already covered by the user's instruction before choosing the route. The route is
advice, not permission or the only allowed next action. Do not invoke it solely
because it was recommended or widen validation or external effects without authorization.

